<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=wind-turbine">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wind turbine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A wind turbine is a device that converts the kinetic energy of wind into electrical energy. The first to generate electricity was installed by the Austrian Josef Friedländer at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> International Electrical Exhibition in 1883: a Halladay wind motor 6.6 m across, whose 3.7 kW drove a dynamo that charged batteries. James Blyth lit his holiday home in Marykirk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, with one in July 1887, and Charles F. Brush built the first automatically operated wind turbine some months later. A 100 kW forerunner of modern horizontal-axis machines ran at Yalta in 1931, and by 2024 turbines generated over 1,136 gigawatts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wind-turbine.jpg" alt="Wind turbine" width="330" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniela Kloth · GFDL 1.2 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2019_07_26_Trampe_Windkraftanlagen_DJI_0041.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The windwheel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hero-of-alexandria">Hero of Alexandria</a>, who lived from about 10 to 70 CE, marks one of the first recorded instances of wind powering a machine. The first known practical wind power plants were built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, an eastern province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, from the seventh century. These panemone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmills</a> were vertical-axle machines with long vertical drive shafts and rectangular blades made of six to twelve sails covered in reed matting or cloth. They were used to grind grain and draw up water, and served in the gristmilling and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> industries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Wind turbines are made in a wide range of sizes, with either horizontal or vertical axes, though horizontal is the most common. Commercial horizontal-axis turbines usually have three blades, placed upwind of the tower, with the rotor shaft and generator at the top, pointed into the wind by a wind sensor and a yaw system. Most have a gearbox that turns the slow rotation of the blades into the quicker rotation suited to a generator, while direct-drive turbines couple the rotor straight to the generator. Vertical-axis designs include the Darrieus, called the eggbeater, the giromill and the Savonius, and they need not be pointed into the wind, though they produce much less energy over time.</p><p>Betz's law sets the maximum extraction of wind power at 16/27, or 59.3 per cent, of the rate at which the kinetic energy of the air arrives at the turbine. Blades are most commonly made of glass fibre composites, and carbon fibre, which is stiffer, stronger and less dense, is also used. The rotor, generator and surrounding structure of tower and yaw mechanism are the three main components, and the turbines have an average lifespan of 30 years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Turbines in large groups, known as wind farms, feed electrical grids, and larger turbines can contribute to a domestic power supply while selling unused power back to the utility through the grid. Smaller turbines serve for battery charging and for remote devices such as traffic warning signs. The power produced varies with the wind, not with demand, so wind is an unreliable source unless energy storage is available.</p><p>A few localities have placed turbines on public display, with visitor centres around their bases or viewing areas farther away, so that they also serve for technology demonstration, public relations and education. The Bahrain World Trade Center has turbines displayed prominently for the public and was the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skyscraper">skyscraper</a> to integrate wind turbines into its design.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wind turbine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1883</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Vienna</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Josef Friedländer</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Wind had powered machines since the windwheel of Hero of Alexandria and the vertical-axle windmills built in Sistan from the 7th century; Friedländer's machine of 1883 was the first to generate electricity.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">Windmill<span>9th century</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/voltaic-pile">Electric battery<span>1800</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/electric-generator">Electric generator<span>1831</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=wind-turbine">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
